## Proof

**Step 1: Establish that $|f| \le g$ almost surely.**

For each $n$, the condition $|f_n| \le g$ holds a.s., so there is a null set $N_n$ with $\mu(N_n)=0$ such that $|f_n(x)| \le g(x)$ for all $x \in M \setminus N_n$. Also, $f_n \to f$ a.s., so there is a null set $N_0$ with $f_n(x) \to f(x)$ for all $x \in M \setminus N_0$. Set
$$N = N_0 \cup \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} N_n,$$
which is null (countable union of null sets). For every $x \in M \setminus N$, we have $|f_n(x)| \le g(x)$ for all $n$ and $f_n(x) \to f(x)$. Since the absolute value is continuous,
$$|f(x)| = \lim_{n\to\infty} |f_n(x)| \le g(x).$$
Hence $|f| \le g$ a.s.

**Step 2: Find an integrable dominating function for $|f_n - f|^p$.**

By the triangle inequality and the bounds from Step 1,
$$|f_n - f| \le |f_n| + |f| \le g + g = 2g \quad \text{a.s.}$$
Raising to the power $p > 0$ (which is monotone on $[0,\infty)$),
$$|f_n - f|^p \le (2g)^p = 2^p g^p \quad \text{a.s.}$$
Since $\int_M g^p\,d\mu < \infty$, the function $h := 2^p g^p$ is integrable, i.e., $h \in L^1(\mu)$.

**Step 3: Apply the Dominated Convergence Theorem.**

Since $f_n \to f$ a.s., the continuity of $t \mapsto |t|^p$ on $\mathbb{R}$ gives
$$|f_n - f|^p \to 0 \quad \text{a.s.}$$
Each $|f_n - f|^p$ is nonnegative and measurable, and by Step 2 it is dominated a.s. by the integrable function $h = 2^p g^p$. The hypotheses of the Dominated Convergence Theorem are therefore satisfied, yielding
$$\lim_{n\to\infty} \int_M |f_n - f|^p\,d\mu = \int_M \lim_{n\to\infty} |f_n - f|^p\,d\mu = \int_M 0\,d\mu = 0.$$

**Conclusion.** We have shown $\displaystyle\int_M |f_n - f|^p\,d\mu \to 0$ as $n \to \infty$. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
